    "https://werkenbij.vu.nl/vacatures/assistant-professor-position-in-evolutionary-adaptation-in-the-anthropocene-amsterdam-1345892": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor position in Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene",
      "description": "Assistant Professor position in Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene | Werken bij VU Vacatures Faculteit/Dienst Faculteiten Academisch Centrum Tandheelkunde Amsterdam (ACTA) Faculteit der Bètawetenschappen Faculteit der Gedrags- en Bewegingswetenschappen Faculteit der  Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen Faculteit der Geneeskunde Faculteit der Rechtsgeleerdheid School of Business and Economics Diensten Bestuurszaken Communicatie & Marketing Dienst Universitaire Relaties & Fondswerving Facilitaire Campus Organisatie Financiën HRM, Arbo & Milieu Informatietechnologie Student- en Onderwijszaken Universiteitsbibliotheek Jong talent PhD Student-assistent Stage Over ons Over ons VU arbeidsvoorwaarden: uitgebreid en flexibel VU persoonlijk: ontwikkelen, erkennen en waarderen VU identiteit: veilig, verantwoord en inclusief VU Campus: groen, rookvrij en sportief VU sollicitatie: proces, screening en participatie Ik ben een kandidaat Ga naar mijn persoonlijke pagina Log in als medewerker Log in 0 NL EN Assistant Professor position in Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene Ga terug O2 Gebouw 0.8 / 1 Assistant Professor Sluit op 02-11-2026 Amsterdam Faculty of Science Postdoc Publicatiedatum 07-10-2026 Solliciteer direct Assistant Professor position in Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene O2 Gebouw Amsterdam 0.8 / 1 Faculty of Science Assistant Professor Postdoc Sluit op 02-11-2026 Publicatiedatum 07-10-2026 Solliciteer direct Are you passionate about research and teaching, and would you like to work in a multidisciplinary team in the field of evolution and ecology? Please apply at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam. Your function The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment (A-LIFE) invites applications for an Assistant Professor in molecular evolution in the field of Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene. Humans are causing unprecedented and fast changes on our planet, challenging scientists to understand how anthropogenic environmental change affects the evolution of traits and interactions of species. Genetic and phenotypic variation play a crucial role in adaptation of species to anthropogenic habitats. We are looking for candidates who combine outstanding research with enthusiastic teaching, in understanding the molecular basis of adaptation in human-impacted environments, using approaches such as epigenetics, transcriptomics, and functional genetics. Candidates studying the molecular effect of selection pressure caused by pollution and/or urbanization on terrestrial invertebrates are specifically invited to apply. The position is embedded in the section Ecology & Evolution, a vibrant research group with a strong global track record in conservation genomics, stress adaptation, species interactions and trait ecology. The candidates are expected to contribute to our focus on integrative ecological and evolutionary research. The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment (A-LIFE) offers a stimulating international research environment with a world-class academic reputation, and state-of-the-art facilities. We have a strong commitment to bachelor and master level teaching. Research in the institute covers a wide range of fundamental and applied research topics with the aim of advancing the fundamental understanding of the interplay between life and its environment. The section’s strength lies in the ability to integrate studies of adaptive changes across the entire range of biological organizational levels, including human populations. We do this as an international, inclusive, and collaborative group of researchers. We ask candidates to write a concise research statement (max. 1 pages) that identifies and presents how their research plans will contribute to the research portfolio of the department in general, and the section Ecology & Evolution in particular. In addition, we ask candidates to provide a concise teaching statement (also 1 pages max.) reflecting on their teaching vision and how they will contribute to the teaching tasks of the department. Your duties develop a vigorous, largely extramurally funded research program teach bachelor and master level courses in evolution, ecology, ecotoxicology or related subjects provide high-quality training and supervision to BSc and MSc-students, PhD-students and postdocs engage in professional development activities related to teaching and academic leadership Your profile PhD in evolutionary biology, molecular biology, ecotoxicology or related field with at least three years of postdoctoral experience strong track record of research and publications in the field of evolutionary adaptation to anthropogenic environmental changes proven ability for acquisition of external funding demonstrated skills in activating teaching methods in BSc and MSc level courses excellent communication skills (written and spoken English) and motivation to work in a team proficiency in Dutch is not required, but the successful candidate is expected to develop at least a passive proficiency within three years after appointment As a university, we strive for equal opportunities for all, recognising that diversity takes many forms. We believe that diversity in all its complexity is invaluable for the quality of our teaching, research and service. We are always looking for talent with diverse backgrounds and experiences. This also means that we are committed to creating an inclusive community so that we can use diversity as an asset. We realise that each individual brings a unique set of skills, expertise and mindset. Therefore we are happy to invite anyone who recognises themselves in the profile to apply, even if you do not meet all the requirements. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of minimum € 4.925,00 (Scale 11) and maximum € 6.682,00 (Scale 11) gross per month, on a full-time basis. This is based on UFO profile Lecturer / Assistant Professor 2. The exact salary depends on your education and experience a position for at least 0.8 FTE. Your employment contract will initially last 1 year. Afterwards, there is a prospect of a permanent contract We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and regulations. Some examples: a full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses hybrid working enables a good work-life balance possibility to save holiday hours, for sabbatical leave About us About the department, The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment ( A-LIFE ) offers a stimulating international research environment with a world-class academic reputation and excellent state-of-the-art facilities. Research in the section Ecology & Evolution covers a wide range of fundamental and applied research topics, including stress ecology, species interactions, evolutionary adaptation, symbiotic interactions, climate change and ecosystem functioning and investigates a variety of animal species. The section’s strength lies in the ability to integrate studies of adaptive changes across the entire range of biological organizational levels. We do this as an international, inclusive, and collaborative group of researchers. Faculty of Science Working at the Faculty of Science means collaborating with students, lecturers and researchers who are focused on their field, yet have a broad view of the world. We are proud of a positive and inclusive workplace culture within the faculty, where we work together with great energy and a pragmatic attitude to tackle social challenges. At the Faculty of Science, scientists and students work on fundamental and complex societal issues for a sustainable, healthy and just future. From forest fires to big data, from obesity to malnutrition, from helium to the universe and from genetics to medicine: our education and research cover the full breadth of science, from molecules to mankind. Our academic education and research are highly experimental, technical and interdisciplinary in nature. That is why we collaborate extensively with leading scientific institutes and industry. The faculty has more than 8,000 students studying in one of the 39 programms and employs more than 1,400 staff across 10 scientific departments, making us one of the largest science faculties in the Netherlands. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Interested Are you interested in this position and do you believe that your experience will contribute to the further development of our university? In that case, we encourage you to submit a cover letter, CV, research statement (max. 1 page), a teaching statement (max. 1 page), and names and contact information for three references by 2 November. References will be contacted for letters of recommendation. Submitting a diploma and a reference check are part of the application process. Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Your function The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment (A-LIFE) invites applications for an Assistant Professor in molecular evolution in the field of Evolutionary Adaptation in the Anthropocene. Humans are causing unprecedented and fast changes on our planet, challenging scientists to understand how anthropogenic environmental change affects the evolution of traits and interactions of species. Genetic and phenotypic variation play a crucial role in adaptation of species to anthropogenic habitats. We are looking for candidates who combine outstanding research with enthusiastic teaching, in understanding the molecular basis of adaptation in human-impacted environments, using approaches such as epigenetics, transcriptomics, and functional genetics. Candidates studying the molecular effect of selection pressure caused by pollution and/or urbanization on terrestrial invertebrates are specifically invited to apply. The position is embedded in the section Ecology & Evolution, a vibrant research group with a strong global track record in conservation genomics, stress adaptation, species interactions and trait ecology. The candidates are expected to contribute to our focus on integrative ecological and evolutionary research. The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment (A-LIFE) offers a stimulating international research environment with a world-class academic reputation, and state-of-the-art facilities. We have a strong commitment to bachelor and master level teaching. Research in the institute covers a wide range of fundamental and applied research topics with the aim of advancing the fundamental understanding of the interplay between life and its environment. The section’s strength lies in the ability to integrate studies of adaptive changes across the entire range of biological organizational levels, including human populations. We do this as an international, inclusive, and collaborative group of researchers. We ask candidates to write a concise research statement (max. 1 pages) that identifies and presents how their research plans will contribute to the research portfolio of the department in general, and the section Ecology & Evolution in particular. In addition, we ask candidates to provide a concise teaching statement (also 1 pages max.) reflecting on their teaching vision and how they will contribute to the teaching tasks of the department. Your duties develop a vigorous, largely extramurally funded research program teach bachelor and master level courses in evolution, ecology, ecotoxicology or related subjects provide high-quality training and supervision to BSc and MSc-students, PhD-students and postdocs engage in professional development activities related to teaching and academic leadership Your profile PhD in evolutionary biology, molecular biology, ecotoxicology or related field with at least three years of postdoctoral experience strong track record of research and publications in the field of evolutionary adaptation to anthropogenic environmental changes proven ability for acquisition of external funding demonstrated skills in activating teaching methods in BSc and MSc level courses excellent communication skills (written and spoken English) and motivation to work in a team proficiency in Dutch is not required, but the successful candidate is expected to develop at least a passive proficiency within three years after appointment As a university, we strive for equal opportunities for all, recognising that diversity takes many forms. We believe that diversity in all its complexity is invaluable for the quality of our teaching, research and service. We are always looking for talent with diverse backgrounds and experiences. This also means that we are committed to creating an inclusive community so that we can use diversity as an asset. We realise that each individual brings a unique set of skills, expertise and mindset. Therefore we are happy to invite anyone who recognises themselves in the profile to apply, even if you do not meet all the requirements. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of minimum € 4.925,00 (Scale 11) and maximum € 6.682,00 (Scale 11) gross per month, on a full-time basis. This is based on UFO profile Lecturer / Assistant Professor 2. The exact salary depends on your education and experience a position for at least 0.8 FTE. Your employment contract will initially last 1 year. Afterwards, there is a prospect of a permanent contract We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and regulations. Some examples: a full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus contribution to commuting expenses hybrid working enables a good work-life balance possibility to save holiday hours, for sabbatical leave About us About the department, The Amsterdam Institute for Life and Environment ( A-LIFE ) offers a stimulating international research environment with a world-class academic reputation and excellent state-of-the-art facilities. Research in the section Ecology & Evolution covers a wide range of fundamental and applied research topics, including stress ecology, species interactions, evolutionary adaptation, symbiotic interactions, climate change and ecosystem functioning and investigates a variety of animal species. The section’s strength lies in the ability to integrate studies of adaptive changes across the entire range of biological organizational levels. We do this as an international, inclusive, and collaborative group of researchers. Faculty of Science Working at the Faculty of Science means collaborating with students, lecturers and researchers who are focused on their field, yet have a broad view of the world. We are proud of a positive and inclusive workplace culture within the faculty, where we work together with great energy and a pragmatic attitude to tackle social challenges. At the Faculty of Science, scientists and students work on fundamental and complex societal issues for a sustainable, healthy and just future. From forest fires to big data, from obesity to malnutrition, from helium to the universe and from genetics to medicine: our education and research cover the full breadth of science, from molecules to mankind. Our academic education and research are highly experimental, technical and interdisciplinary in nature. That is why we collaborate extensively with leading scientific institutes and industry. The faculty has more than 8,000 students studying in one of the 39 programms and employs more than 1,400 staff across 10 scientific departments, making us one of the largest science faculties in the Netherlands. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met Prof.dr. M.P. Berg Section head Ecology & Evolution 0612530372 Stuur een e-mail Interested Are you interested in this position and do you believe that your experience will contribute to the further development of our university? In that case, we encourage you to submit a cover letter, CV, research statement (max. 1 page), a teaching statement (max. 1 page), and names and contact information for three references by 2 November. References will be contacted for letters of recommendation. Submitting a diploma and a reference check are part of the application process. Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Vragen over de vacature? Neem c"
    },
    "https://werkenbij.vu.nl/vacatures/phd-position-in-space-law-and-artificial-intelligence-amsterdam-1346045": {
      "title": "PhD Position in Space Law and Artificial Intelligence",
      "description": "PhD Position in Space Law and Artificial Intelligence | Werken bij VU Vacatures Faculteit/Dienst Faculteiten Academisch Centrum Tandheelkunde Amsterdam (ACTA) Faculteit der Bètawetenschappen Faculteit der Gedrags- en Bewegingswetenschappen Faculteit der  Sociale en Geesteswetenschappen Faculteit der Geneeskunde Faculteit der Rechtsgeleerdheid School of Business and Economics Diensten Bestuurszaken Communicatie & Marketing Dienst Universitaire Relaties & Fondswerving Facilitaire Campus Organisatie Financiën HRM, Arbo & Milieu Informatietechnologie Student- en Onderwijszaken Universiteitsbibliotheek Jong talent PhD Student-assistent Stage Over ons Over ons VU arbeidsvoorwaarden: uitgebreid en flexibel VU persoonlijk: ontwikkelen, erkennen en waarderen VU identiteit: veilig, verantwoord en inclusief VU Campus: groen, rookvrij en sportief VU sollicitatie: proces, screening en participatie Ik ben een kandidaat Ga naar mijn persoonlijke pagina Log in als medewerker Log in 0 NL EN PhD Position in Space Law and Artificial Intelligence Ga terug Initium Gebouw 0.8 / 1 Sluit op 27-11-2026 Amsterdam Faculty of Law Publicatiedatum 07-10-2026 Solliciteer direct PhD Position in Space Law and Artificial Intelligence Initium Gebouw Amsterdam 0.8 / 1 Faculty of Law Sluit op 27-11-2026 Publicatiedatum 07-10-2026 Solliciteer direct Who sets the rules when AI turns Earth Observation data into decisions about security, resilience and society? Join us as a PhD researcher and help shape the unique intersection of space law and artificial intelligence. Your function You will conduct doctoral research within EO-TRUST , a €1 million multidisciplinary project funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO), led by Dr Ioana Bratu. Your research will examine the legal and ethical dimensions of AI-enabled Earth Observation, focusing on how space law, AI regulation and related legal frameworks address questions of resilience, responsibility and trustworthy use of satellite data. You will work in a multidisciplinary environment connecting law with Earth Observation, artificial intelligence and computer science. Your duties conduct independent doctoral research on the legal and ethical governance of AI-enabled Earth Observation map and analyse applicable legal frameworks, including international space law, the EU Space Act, the EU AI Act and relevant security and military law conduct doctrinal and empirical research, including interviews and engagement with governmental, regulatory, academic and industry stakeholders contribute to academic publications, policy outputs, conferences, workshops and the development of principles for responsible and explainable AI-enabled Earth Observation contribute to the activities of Amsterdam Law & Technology Institute and Amsterdam Space Law & Sustainability Center . Your profile a completed LL.M. or equivalent master's degree in law, preferably with a specialisation or demonstrated interest in technology (AI) law, space law, EU law or public international law strong academic research and legal analytical skills, with an interest in collaborating in a multidisciplinary environment knowledge of, or a demonstrable interest in, the legal and regulatory implications of space activities and artificial intelligence experience or an interest in empirical legal research and in working with stakeholders from government, industry and technical disciplines excellent written and spoken English and the ability to communicate legal research clearly to both academic and non-academic audiences As a university, we strive for equal opportunities for all, recognising that diversity takes many forms. We believe that diversity in all its complexity is invaluable for the quality of our teaching, research and service. We are always looking for talent with diverse backgrounds and experiences. This also means that we are committed to creating an inclusive community so that we can use diversity as an asset. We realise that each individual brings a unique set of skills, expertise and mindset. Therefore we are happy to invite anyone who recognises themselves in the profile to apply, even if you do not meet all the requirements. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of minimum € 3.204 (Scale 85 PhD.) and maximum € 4.051 (Scale 85 PhD.) gross per month, on a full-time basis. This is based on UFO profile PhD candidate. The exact salary depends on your education and experience. a position for at least 0.8 FTE. Your employment contract will initially last 1 year. Afterwards, the possibility of expansion to a total of 4 years. the possibility to engage and collaborate with industry and governmental stakeholders as part of the EO-TRUST project. For example, you will have the opportunity to gain hands-on industry expertise at Caeli BV, as an intern. you will benefit from international insights offered by a renowned Advisory Board, as part of the EO-TRUST project. Members include representatives from ESA, the European Commission, EUSPA, the European Parliament, top AI policy & governmental advisors and space law advisors and academics. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and regulations. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus solid pension scheme (ABP) a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge contribution to commuting expenses About us Amsterdam Space Law and Sustainability Institute Hosted at the Faculty of Law of VU Amsterdam and funded by the Amsterdam Law & Technology Institute (ALTI), the Amsterdam Space Law & Sustainability Center is a multidisciplinary platform for research and collaboration on the legal, technological and societal challenges of human activities in outer space. Its research focuses on areas including space sustainability, security and militarisation, artificial intelligence, quantum technologies and other critical technologies, as well as the legal dimensions of space operations. Faculty of Law At the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam’s Faculty of Law we train lawyers and criminologists to develop sharp critical minds, with a strong focus on the societal relevance of law. We offer Bachelor’s programmes in four main fields: Law, Notarial Law, Law in Society and Criminology. In addition, we offer an extensive range of Master’s programmes as well as contract education (for non-student visitors). Much of our research is both international and multidisciplinary in character. Our teaching and research embody the values of cooperation and diversity. We encourage each other to view problems from different perspectives, to think critically, develop a broader mind and arrive at creative legal solutions together. Are you interested in joining the Faculty of Law? You will work in an active and inspiring academic environment. Together with your colleagues, you will contribute to the excellence of teaching and research in a pleasant and collegial working environment. You will join over 400 staff members responsible for a student body of 4,500. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Interested Are you interested? Please submit your CV and a relevant writing sample (e.g., master thesis, thesis chapter or publication) by Monday November 16 (23:59 hr). Interviews (in Amsterdam or online) are expected to take place between 1 December and 11 December. References may be requested as part of the application process. The start date of the position is 1 February 2027. For questions about the position, please contact Dr Ioana Bratu, [email protected] . Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Your function You will conduct doctoral research within EO-TRUST , a €1 million multidisciplinary project funded by the Dutch Research Council (NWO), led by Dr Ioana Bratu. Your research will examine the legal and ethical dimensions of AI-enabled Earth Observation, focusing on how space law, AI regulation and related legal frameworks address questions of resilience, responsibility and trustworthy use of satellite data. You will work in a multidisciplinary environment connecting law with Earth Observation, artificial intelligence and computer science. Your duties conduct independent doctoral research on the legal and ethical governance of AI-enabled Earth Observation map and analyse applicable legal frameworks, including international space law, the EU Space Act, the EU AI Act and relevant security and military law conduct doctrinal and empirical research, including interviews and engagement with governmental, regulatory, academic and industry stakeholders contribute to academic publications, policy outputs, conferences, workshops and the development of principles for responsible and explainable AI-enabled Earth Observation contribute to the activities of Amsterdam Law & Technology Institute and Amsterdam Space Law & Sustainability Center . Your profile a completed LL.M. or equivalent master's degree in law, preferably with a specialisation or demonstrated interest in technology (AI) law, space law, EU law or public international law strong academic research and legal analytical skills, with an interest in collaborating in a multidisciplinary environment knowledge of, or a demonstrable interest in, the legal and regulatory implications of space activities and artificial intelligence experience or an interest in empirical legal research and in working with stakeholders from government, industry and technical disciplines excellent written and spoken English and the ability to communicate legal research clearly to both academic and non-academic audiences As a university, we strive for equal opportunities for all, recognising that diversity takes many forms. We believe that diversity in all its complexity is invaluable for the quality of our teaching, research and service. We are always looking for talent with diverse backgrounds and experiences. This also means that we are committed to creating an inclusive community so that we can use diversity as an asset. We realise that each individual brings a unique set of skills, expertise and mindset. Therefore we are happy to invite anyone who recognises themselves in the profile to apply, even if you do not meet all the requirements. What do we offer? A challenging position in a socially engaged organisation. At VU Amsterdam, you contribute to education, research and service for a better world. And that is valuable. So in return for your efforts, we offer you: a salary of minimum € 3.204 (Scale 85 PhD.) and maximum € 4.051 (Scale 85 PhD.) gross per month, on a full-time basis. This is based on UFO profile PhD candidate. The exact salary depends on your education and experience. a position for at least 0.8 FTE. Your employment contract will initially last 1 year. Afterwards, the possibility of expansion to a total of 4 years. the possibility to engage and collaborate with industry and governmental stakeholders as part of the EO-TRUST project. For example, you will have the opportunity to gain hands-on industry expertise at Caeli BV, as an intern. you will benefit from international insights offered by a renowned Advisory Board, as part of the EO-TRUST project. Members include representatives from ESA, the European Commission, EUSPA, the European Parliament, top AI policy & governmental advisors and space law advisors and academics. We also offer you attractive fringe benefits and regulations. Some examples: A full-time 38-hour working week comes with a holiday leave entitlement of 232 hours per year. If you choose to work 40 hours, you have 96 extra holiday leave hours on an annual basis. For part-timers, this is calculated pro rata. 8% holiday allowance and 8.3% end-of-year bonus solid pension scheme (ABP) a wide range of sports facilities which staff may use at a modest charge contribution to commuting expenses About us Amsterdam Space Law and Sustainability Institute Hosted at the Faculty of Law of VU Amsterdam and funded by the Amsterdam Law & Technology Institute (ALTI), the Amsterdam Space Law & Sustainability Center is a multidisciplinary platform for research and collaboration on the legal, technological and societal challenges of human activities in outer space. Its research focuses on areas including space sustainability, security and militarisation, artificial intelligence, quantum technologies and other critical technologies, as well as the legal dimensions of space operations. Faculty of Law At the Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam’s Faculty of Law we train lawyers and criminologists to develop sharp critical minds, with a strong focus on the societal relevance of law. We offer Bachelor’s programmes in four main fields: Law, Notarial Law, Law in Society and Criminology. In addition, we offer an extensive range of Master’s programmes as well as contract education (for non-student visitors). Much of our research is both international and multidisciplinary in character. Our teaching and research embody the values of cooperation and diversity. We encourage each other to view problems from different perspectives, to think critically, develop a broader mind and arrive at creative legal solutions together. Are you interested in joining the Faculty of Law? You will work in an active and inspiring academic environment. Together with your colleagues, you will contribute to the excellence of teaching and research in a pleasant and collegial working environment. You will join over 400 staff members responsible for a student body of 4,500. Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam stands for values-driven education and research. We are open-minded experts with the ability to think freely - a broader mind. Maintaining an entrepreneurial perspective and concentrating on diversity, significance and humanity, we work on sustainable solutions with social impact. By joining forces, across the boundaries of disciplines, we work towards a better world for people and planet. Together we create a safe and respectful working and study climate, and an inspiring environment for education and research. Learn more about our codes of conduct We are located on one physical campus, in the heart of Amsterdam's Zuidas business district, with excellent location and accessibility.  Over 6,150 staff work at the VU and over 31,000 students attend academic education. Diversity Diversity is the driving force of VU Amsterdam. VU wants to be accessible and receptive to diversity in disciplines, cultures, ideas, nationalities, beliefs, preferences and worldviews. We believe that trust, respect, interest and differences lead to new insights and innovation, to sharpness and clarity, to excellence and a broader understanding. We stand for an inclusive community and believe that diversity and internationalisation contribute to the quality of education, research and our services. Therefore, we are always searching for people whose backgrounds and experience contribute to the diversity of the VU community. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met Ioana Bratu Assistant Professor Space Law & AI Stuur een e-mail Interested Are you interested? Please submit your CV and a relevant writing sample (e.g., master thesis, thesis chapter or publication) by Monday November 16 (23:59 hr). Interviews (in Amsterdam or online) are expected to take place between 1 December and 11 December. References may be requested as part of the application process. The start date of the position is 1 February 2027. For questions about the position, please contact Dr Ioana Bratu, [email protected] . Applications received by e-mail will not be considered. Acquisition in response to this advertisement is not appreciated. Vragen over de vacature? Neem contact op met Ioana Bratu Assistant Professor Space Law & AI Stuur een e-mail Sollicitatieprocedure Andere interessante vacatures Docent straf- en strafprocesrecht 0,8 / 1 Faculteit der Rechtsgeleerdheid Docent LL.M Sluit op 26-10-2026 Publicatiedatum 08-10-2026 0.0 km De afdeling Strafrecht van de Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam zoekt een docent Straf- en Strafprocesrecht. Ben ji